window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_b1-621b1f934f814a63"]={"key":"ny_b1-621b1f934f814a63","group":"ny_b1","uid":"621b1f934f814a63","rank":85,"title":"brantham records exemption position memo","occupation":"lawyers","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_b1","prompt":"You are Perpetua Lindgren, counsel to the Brantham Interstate Water Compact's oversight commission. The commission covers nine member states but has never adopted a compact-level public-records policy, so staff have been answering requests ad hoc and the same kind of document has received different treatment depending on who handled it. The commission now needs a usable memorandum before its next public-records training session. The supplied file `brantham_public_records_authorities.docx` contains the nine state records-act extracts, four attorney-general opinions, and two appellate decisions concerning the compact's confidentiality clause; use it as the authority set rather than researching outside sources. Prepare `brantham_records_exemption_position_memo.docx` and write it into the top level of the working directory the solver starts in, using exactly that filename. The memorandum is for frontline records staff and their supervisors, not for litigators, and must be no more than six pages. Set out, exemption by exemption, what each member state permits or does not permit, identify material changes or differences across the two comparison periods covered by the source materials, and give the compact a single defensible position when state approaches diverge. Staff particularly need a clear treatment of requests involving draft modeling outputs, attorney-client or work-product material, personnel and medical information, security-sensitive infrastructure information, trade secrets, and records supplied by other public bodies. Explain what staff can decide under this memorandum and where a request must be escalated to counsel or the commission's records administrator; do not turn the memorandum into a records-management manual, litigation strategy, or technical system guide. The commission also works with Cobblemoor Mutual and Brightcastle Health Network on water-resilience projects, so make clear how records received from those organisations are treated without implying that either organisation controls the compact's disclosure decision. Use plain language, define any term that frontline staff might reasonably misunderstand, cite the supplied authorities precisely enough for a reviewer to locate them, distinguish a legal requirement from a recommended practice, and end with the commission's position rather than a list of future tasks.\n\n---\n\nThe files referred to above are in `references/`:\n\n- `references/brantham_public_records_authorities.docx` — Single supplied authority bundle containing the nine member-state records-act extracts, four attorney-general opinions, two appellate decisions interpreting the compact confidentiality clause, comparison-period indicators, and the factual material needed to reconcile conflicting treatment of records and exemptions.\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `brantham_records_exemption_position_memo.docx` — A completed records-exemption survey and unified position memorandum for frontline Brantham Interstate Water Compact records staff and supervisors. (Must be no more than six pages when rendered with ordinary readable document formatting.; Must use the supplied authority bundle as its source and identify authorities precisely enough to locate them.; Must cover all nine member states, both comparison periods, the compact-level confidentiality issue, and the stated exemption subjects.; Must distinguish mandatory legal requirements, discretionary permissions, and recommended internal practice.; Must identify the boundary between frontline staff decisions and matters requiring escalation.; Must not present records-management procedures, litigation strategy, technical system instructions, or unsupported legal conclusions.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[{"name":"package/public/reference_files/brantham_public_records_authorities.docx","bytes":42163,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/1679b26628cfd2f28785d66415412c659fb524e9ea0703df4837c6e733d71728.docx","sha256":"1679b26628cfd2f28785d66415412c659fb524e9ea0703df4837c6e733d71728","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"BRANTHAM PUBLIC-RECORDS AUTHORITIES\nInternal authority bundle • prepared 12 August 2024\nPurpose. This bundle assembles the extracts used for the Commission’s public-records training. It is self-contained; citations in the position memorandum use the section and page markers below. It is not a policy or legal opinion.\nComparison periods. Period 1 (P1) is the 15 January 2021 statutory baseline. Period 2 (P2) is the 30 June 2024 text after the amendments and interpretations identified in this bundle. “P1” and “P2” in a citation refer to those snapshots.\nA. Member-state act extracts\nAll nine acts begin with a presumption that records of a public body are open. Each places the justification for withholding on the agency and requires disclosure of reasonably segregable nonexempt material. The extracts below are condensed but verbatim in operative effect; bracketed page markers are stable bundle locators.\nState / act locator\nGeneral rule\nDeliberative process\nAttorney / work product\nPersonnel / medical\nInfrastructure security\nTrade secret / commercial\nAlderfen\\nAlderfen Open Records Act §12-101 (P1 p.2; P2 p.2)\nOpen unless a specific exemption applies; agency must identify the exemption.\nP1/P2: §12-114 permits withholding of predecisional recommendations and drafts; segregable factual material remains open.\nP1/P2: §12-117 protects confidential legal advice and attorney work product prepared for the agency; ordinary policy emails are not covered.\nP1: personnel files protected only where disclosure is a clearly unwarranted invasion; medical details redacted. P2: §12-121 makes medical information confidential, personnel privacy balancing unchanged.\nP1/P2: §12-119 protects vulnerability assessments, access controls, and detailed water-system security plans; general locations and budgets open.\nP1/P2: §12-122 protects trade secrets and commercial data only when independently secret, competitively valuable, and supplied under a written confidentiality request.\nBellwether\\nBellwether Records Act §4-201 (P1 p.3; P2 p.3)\nAccess is the rule; custodian bears burden and must cite a subsection.\nP1/P2: §4-208 covers interagency recommendations made before a decision; factual studies are not deliberative.\nP1: §4-210 protects attorney-client communications. P2 amendment adds work product, but only litigation-oriented analysis; protection is permissive (“may withhold”).\nP1/P2: employee home contact and medical information may be redacted; disciplinary findings are generally open after final action.\nP1/P2: §4-213 protects operational schematics and cyber-response details whose release would facilitate attack; not routine maintenance records.\nP1/P2: §4-214 requires proof of secrecy measures and likely competitive harm; submitter label is evidence, not conclusive.\nCedarvale\\nCedarvale Transparency Code §7-301 (P1 p.4; P2 p.4)\nPublic-body records are presumed open; exemptions are narrowly read and the body must justify each redaction.\nP1/P2: §7-309 expressly protects recommendations, options, and evaluative drafts before final action; raw data must be separated.\nP1/P2: §7-312 covers confidential advice from counsel and litigation work product; copying advice to non-client vendors may waive protection under §7-312(c).\nP1/P2: §7-315 protects medical information and intimate personal facts; personnel evaluations are protected only while candid and current, then subject to balancing.\nP1/P2: §7-318 protects specific vulnerabilities in dams, treatment plants, and control networks; high-level resilience plans are open.\nP1: §7-320 protects trade secrets if owner demonstrates secrecy and value. P2 adds notice to the owner but does not make notice dispositive.\nDunshire\\nDunshire Public Information Act §9-401 (P1 p.5; P2 p.5)\nRecords are available on request; the agency must make a particularized showing for an exemption.\nNo deliberative-process exemption in P1 or P2. §9-407 says draft status alone is not a basis to withhold.\nP1/P2: §9-409 protects attorney-client communications and work product only if kept confidential and tied to legal advice or litigation; business advice is open.\nP1/P2: §9-412 protects medical information; ordinary personnel records are open except personal identifiers and medical details.\nP1/P2: §9-414 protects security plans and vulnerability details, but not factual incident logs after the threat passes.\nP1/P2: §9-416 protects trade secrets and confidential commercial information when secrecy, value, and likely harm are shown; agency decides, not submitter.\nEastmere\\nEastmere Access Act §3-501 (P1 p.6; P2 p.6)\nOpen by default; withholding requires a cited statutory ground and a segregability review.\nP1: §3-508 protects predecisional policy analysis. P2 narrows it to recommendations still under active consideration; completed recommendations are open.\nP1/P2: §3-510 protects legal advice and work product; a lawyer’s presence is not enough.\nP1/P2: §3-512 protects medical information and narrowly defined personal identifiers; final discipline and salary ranges open.\nP1/P2: §3-514 protects detailed security assessments and exploit information; generic infrastructure descriptions open.\nP1/P2: §3-516 protects secret formulas, pricing, and methods if owner takes reasonable measures and harm is likely; designation must be specific.\nFoxgrove\\nFoxgrove Public Records Act §5-601 (P1 p.7; P2 p.7)\nAccess is presumed; agency bears the justification burden and cannot rely on a blanket category.\nP1/P2: §5-607 protects staff recommendations and draft options linked to a pending decision; factual appendices are open.\nP1/P2: §5-609 protects privileged communications and work product; disclosure to a project partner outside the legal team can waive it.\nP1/P2: §5-611 protects medical records and personal financial identifiers; personnel evaluations are conditionally protected while active.\nP1/P2: §5-613 protects information that reveals a specific exploitable weakness in critical water infrastructure.\nP1/P2: §5-615 protects trade secrets only upon a showing of secrecy and competitive injury; a confidentiality stamp alone is insufficient.\nGreyhaven\\nGreyhaven Public Access Act §8-701 (P1 p.8; P2 p.8)\nPublic records are open unless the custodian proves a defined exemption; partial release is required where feasible.\nP1/P2: §8-708 protects predecisional deliberations and draft recommendations; objective factual material must be released.\nP1: §8-710 protects attorney-client communications. P2 opinion AG-24-03 reads §8-710 to include work product, but the text remains permissive.\nP1/P2: §8-712 protects medical information and personal identifiers; general employment history is open.\nP1/P2: §8-714 protects detailed maps, access credentials, and vulnerability information; summary resilience goals are open.\nP1/P2: §8-716 protects confidential commercial information when the owner proves secrecy and substantial competitive harm; agency must test the proof.\nHartsfield\\nHartsfield Government Records Act §2-801 (P1 p.9; P2 p.9)\nRecords are presumed public; an exemption must be specific, limited, and supported by the agency.\nP1/P2: §2-806 protects deliberative recommendations and draft policy positions, but not purely factual compilations.\nP1/P2: §2-808 protects legal advice and work product; waiver follows disclosure to persons outside the representation.\nP1/P2: §2-810 protects medical and personally identifying information; personnel files receive no blanket exemption.\nP1/P2: §2-812 protects security-sensitive designs, response playbooks, and vulnerability findings; ordinary project status is open.\nP1/P2: §2-814 protects trade secrets and confidential commercial information on a specific showing of secrecy and harm; submitter request is not controlling.\nIronwood\\nIronwood Public Records Code §6-901 (P1 p.10; P2 p.10)\nOpen access is presumed and any withholding must be tied to the code; segregable portions must be released.\nP1/P2: §6-907 protects predecisional analyses and recommendations; drafts containing only measurements are not covered.\nP1/P2: §6-909 protects attorney-client and work-product material maintained confidentially; routine project communications remain open.\nP1/P2: §6-911 protects medical information and narrowly tailored privacy interests; final employee discipline is open.\nP1/P2: §6-913 protects information whose release would expose a specific vulnerability in water or control infrastructure; high-level plans open.\nP1/P2: §6-915 protects trade secrets where secrecy, value, and likely competitive harm are established; label alone does not decide.\nReading note: “may withhold” is permissive; “must protect/confidential” is mandatory within the stated conditions. All extracts require segregation. The act excerpts are binding statutory text for their state; they do not by themselves dictate the Commission’s compact-level rule.\nB. Attorney-general opinions (persuasive)\nAG-21-02 (Alderfen, 3 Mar 2021), pp.11–12. A draft model is a record when it has been used to communicate an agency position or support a decision; draft label alone does not remove it from the act. Deliberative protection may still cover recommendations, not underlying measurements.\nAG-22-07 (Dunshire, 18 Oct 2022), pp.13–14. Construes §9-407 narrowly: a working file that never leaves the analyst’s control and contains no adopted communication is not a public record. Once transmitted for agency decision, it is a record; Dunshire has no deliberative exemption.\nAG-23-05 (Cedarvale, 9 May 2023), pp.15–16. A confidentiality stamp from a vendor is not dispositive. The agency must test statutory secrecy, value, and harm; redact only the qualifying commercial portion.\nAG-24-03 (Greyhaven, 22 Apr 2024), pp.17–18. Reads §8-710 to include attorney work product, but describes the protection as discretionary and warns that business advice is not privileged. Opinion is persuasive, not binding outside Greyhaven.\nC. Appellate decisions on Compact confidentiality clause\nRillwater Commission v. Northbank Press, 41 B.I.W.C. App. 18 (P1, 16 Sept 2021), pp.19–21 (Northbank). Holding: the clause (“information furnished to the Commission in confidence shall be protected from disclosure”) is an independent compact promise. For material genuinely furnished in confidence, the Commission must withhold even where a member-state act would otherwise permit access. The court stressed the negotiated interstate text and the word “shall.” It did not decide every record category; its discussion of state exemptions is explanatory dicta.\nSouth Fork Gazette v. Brantham Commission, 47 B.I.W.C. App. 77 (P2, 2 Feb 2024), pp.22–24 (South Fork). Holding: the clause is a direction to apply the governing disclosure law, not a blanket exemption. “In confidence” describes handling, while “shall be protected” operates only where a state-law exemption or other lawful ground exists. The court relied on the compact’s savings language and warned that an agency cannot create secrecy by labeling a record confidential. Its statement that Northbank overread the clause is binding only in the South Fork appeal; broader reconciliation is explanatory.\nConflict marker. Both decisions assume genuine confidential submission, but assign different work to the same clause: Northbank treats the clause as an independent mandatory shield; South Fork treats it as conditional on an underlying lawful exemption. The textual pull is “shall/protected” versus the savings language and anti-labeling concern. The bundle provides no later en banc resolution.\nD. Third-party project records\nCobblemoor Mutual supplied flood-loss scenarios and pricing assumptions under a written confidentiality request (P1 file note, p.25). Brightcastle Health Network supplied de-identified health-impact summaries and a redacted patient-safety annex for the resilience project (P2 file note, p.26). The notes state that the Commission retains custody and disclosure authority; neither organisation may veto a request. Apply the relevant statutory commercial, privacy, security, or privilege test to the content. Provenance alone is not an exemption.\nBundle citation key: cite the act as “State Act §section (P1/P2, bundle p.x)”; opinions as “AG-year-number (bundle pp.x–y)”; cases by short name and reporter/page (bundle pp.x–y).","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/brantham_records_exemption_position_memo.docx","bytes":42451,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/2ececbd11f151e7f99003ed391b5366dadc284509dbfc133b61112706116fd49.docx","sha256":"2ececbd11f151e7f99003ed391b5366dadc284509dbfc133b61112706116fd49","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"BRANTHAM INTERSTATE WATER COMPACT\nRecords-Exemption Position Memorandum\nTo: Frontline records staff and supervisors  |  From: Perpetua Lindgren, Counsel  |  12 August 2024\nPurpose and scope. This is the Commission’s working legal position for handling access requests. It is written for frontline staff, not litigators. It applies the supplied Brantham Public-Records Authorities bundle only (the nine act extracts, four persuasive AG opinions, and two appellate decisions); no outside research was used. It addresses disclosure analysis, not retention, records-management administration, litigation strategy, or technical security controls.\nHow to read “must” and “should.” “Must” states a legal requirement shown by the bundle. “May” describes a statutory permission, not an instruction to withhold. “Should” is the Commission’s recommended practice. “Record” means recorded information in the Commission’s custody that documents public business; a draft can still be a record. “Exemption” is a legal ground allowing or requiring withholding. “Redaction” removes only protected content; “segregation” means releasing reasonably separable nonexempt parts.\n1. Baseline and decision boundary\nAll nine states start from openness and place the justification burden on the public body. A withholding decision must cite a defined ground, be no broader than necessary, and release reasonably segregable material. A permissive exemption (“may withhold”) does not make withholding mandatory. Staff may apply a clear, single-ground request when the record and redaction are straightforward. Escalate to the records administrator (RA) for mixed records, partial-release judgment, fee/notice questions, or uncertain record status; escalate to counsel for privilege, the compact clause, novel claims, threatened dispute, or a cross-state conflict.\n2. State survey (P1 baseline → P2, 2021 → 2024)\nThe table is a practical locator, not a substitute for the cited extract. “DP” means deliberative process. All entries incorporate each act’s segregation duty.\nState / starting rule\nDP?\nAttorney / work product\nPersonnel / medical\nSecurity-sensitive infrastructure\nTrade secret / commercial\nP1 → P2 material change\nAlderfen (§12-101, bundle p.2)\nYes: §12-114; factual portions open.\n§12-117 legal advice/work product; business email open.\nP1 balancing; P2 §12-121 makes medical confidential.\n§12-119 vulnerability/access plans; general plans open.\n§12-122 secrecy + value + written request.\nMedical protection became mandatory in P2.\nBellwether (§4-201, p.3)\nYes: §4-208; factual studies open.\nP1 advice; P2 adds litigation work product; permissive.\nRedact home contact/medical; final discipline open.\n§4-213 exploit-enabling schematics/cyber detail only.\n§4-214 secrecy + likely harm; label not conclusive.\nP2 work-product amendment, limited to litigation analysis.\nCedarvale (§7-301, p.4)\nYes: §7-309; raw data segregated.\n§7-312 advice/work product; vendor copying may waive.\n§7-315 medical/intimate facts; current evaluations conditional.\n§7-318 specific vulnerabilities; high-level goals open.\nP1 §7-320 proof; P2 adds owner notice, not dispositive.\nP2 notice step is procedural, not automatic secrecy.\nDunshire (§9-401, p.5)\nNo DP exemption; §9-407 says draft alone is not enough.\n§9-409 confidential legal advice/work product tied to law/litigation.\n§9-412 medical + identifiers; ordinary personnel open.\n§9-414 plans/vulnerabilities; post-threat incident logs open.\n§9-416 secrecy + value + likely harm; agency decides.\nNo DP protection in either period—the exceptional state.\nEastmere (§3-501, p.6)\nP1 broad §3-508; P2 only active-consideration recommendations.\n§3-510; lawyer presence alone insufficient.\n§3-512 medical/limited identifiers; final discipline/salary ranges open.\n§3-514 detailed assessments/exploits; generic descriptions open.\n§3-516 measures + likely harm; specific designation.\nP2 narrows DP to live decisions.\nFoxgrove (§5-601, p.7)\n§5-607 pending-decision options; factual appendices open.\n§5-609; outside-team disclosure can waive.\n§5-611 medical/financial IDs; active evaluations conditional.\n§5-613 specific exploitable weakness.\n§5-615 secrecy + competitive injury; stamp insufficient.\nNo material P2 text change.\nGreyhaven (§8-701, p.8)\n§8-708 predecisional drafts; objective facts open.\nP1 §8-710 advice; P2 AG-24-03 persuasive work-product reading.\n§8-712 medical/IDs; employment history open.\n§8-714 maps/credentials/vulnerabilities; summary goals open.\n§8-716 secrecy + substantial harm; agency tests proof.\nP2 opinion expands interpretation but is not binding statute.\nHartsfield (§2-801, p.9)\n§2-806 recommendations/draft positions; factual compilations open.\n§2-808; waiver outside representation.\n§2-810 medical/PII; no blanket personnel exemption.\n§2-812 designs/playbooks/findings; status open.\n§2-814 specific secrecy + harm; request not controlling.\nNo material P2 text change.\nIronwood (§6-901, p.10)\n§6-907 analyses/recommendations; measurements open.\n§6-909 confidential advice/work product; project comms open.\n§6-911 medical/narrow privacy; final discipline open.\n§6-913 specific vulnerability; high-level plans open.\n§6-915 secrecy + value + likely harm; label alone fails.\nNo material P2 text change.\nPeriod comparison in one sentence: P2 materially changes Alderfen medical privacy, Bellwether work product, Cedarvale commercial notice, Eastmere’s live-decision DP scope, and Greyhaven’s persuasive work-product reading; the remaining states’ operative text is stable. Cite the P1/P2 page markers above for review.\n3. Subject-by-subject application\nDraft modeling outputs. AG-21-02 treats a draft as a record once used to communicate a position or support a decision; AG-22-07 uses a narrower “never left the analyst’s control/no adopted communication” concept. The Commission adopts the narrower operational rule for intake only as a status screen: a purely private working file not communicated for Commission business is outside the request; once circulated, relied on, or attached to a decision, treat it as a record. Record status does not itself authorize withholding. Apply DP only to predecisional, deliberative recommendations/options (not raw measurements or factual appendices), and release separable facts. Dunshire’s lack of DP means its drafts need another lawful ground. Draft label alone is never enough. (AG-21-02 pp.11–12; AG-22-07 pp.13–14; Dunshire §9-407 p.5.)\nAttorney-client/work product. Protect confidential communications seeking or giving legal advice and litigation work product only where the state test is met; a lawyer’s name or attendance is not dispositive. Ordinary program, policy, pricing, or engineering communications remain open. Disclosure outside the privileged representation can waive protection under Cedarvale §7-312(c), Foxgrove §5-609, and Hartsfield §2-808; do not assume a vendor is within that relationship. Staff should route any privilege claim or possible waiver to counsel.\nPersonnel and medical information. No state makes every employee record exempt. Protect medical information and specified personal identifiers; apply the source’s narrower balancing or active-evaluation limits to personnel material. Redact the protected datum and release the job, date, decision, or other separable public content. A mixed file goes to the RA; a medical or sensitive personnel dispute goes to counsel.\nSecurity-sensitive infrastructure. Withhold only details tied to a concrete security interest—vulnerability findings, access credentials, exploit-enabling schematics, response playbooks, or equivalent source-defined detail. General locations, budgets, high-level resilience goals, and post-threat factual logs are not automatically protected. Escalate if the harm nexus is not obvious; technical controls are outside this memo.\nTrade secrets and commercial information. The recurring test is independent secrecy, commercial value, and likely competitive harm (plus any state-specific written request, notice, or reasonable-measures condition). A submitter’s confidentiality stamp or request is evidence, not the decision. Redact only qualifying formulas, pricing, methods, or assumptions and release the rest. AG-23-05 confirms that owner notice does not make the claim conclusive (pp.15–16).\n4. Compact confidentiality clause and third parties\nThe appellate conflict is real. Northbank Press (41 B.I.W.C. App. 18, P1, pp.19–21) holds that “information furnished…in confidence shall be protected” is an independent mandatory compact shield; its discussion of state exemptions is dicta. South Fork Gazette (47 B.I.W.C. App. 77, P2, pp.22–24) holds the clause directs application of a lawful state exemption, with “in confidence” describing handling; it rejects label-created secrecy and relies on savings language. The textual tension is “shall/protected” versus savings language and anti-labeling. Neither case resolves every category, and no later en banc resolution appears in the bundle.\nCommission position. For Commission records, apply a defined state-law or other lawful exemption and the segregation rule; do not withhold solely because a record is stamped confidential or because Northbank’s broader reading is available. The clause reinforces careful handling and may require protection where a valid exemption applies, but it does not erase Dunshire’s absent DP exemption or create a blanket shield. If a state law would permit access and the clause is the only asserted ground, escalate to counsel/RA rather than label the record exempt. This narrower position is defensible under South Fork, respects Northbank’s mandatory reading as a material risk, and avoids selecting the broadest exemption.\nCobblemoor Mutual and Brightcastle Health Network. Their source status does not decide disclosure. The Commission owns the response. Apply content-based tests: Cobblemoor’s flood scenarios/pricing may qualify under commercial-information conditions; Brightcastle’s de-identified health summaries may be open while a patient-safety annex may require privacy or security analysis. Neither organisation can veto release; their confidentiality request is not conclusive. Escalate any mixed or disputed third-party claim.\n5. Frontline decisions and escalation\nStaff may, without counsel, release clearly public factual material; apply an obvious, narrow redaction for a specified medical identifier, home contact, credential, or plainly segregable commercial secret; and release factual portions of a deliberative or mixed record when the cited extract clearly requires segregation. Record the applicable bundle locator in the response. These are bounded decisions, not a records-management workflow.\nMust escalate to the RA or counsel when: record status is disputed (especially a draft model); privilege or waiver is claimed; a request invokes the compact clause alone; a state approach conflicts; a whole mixed record is proposed for withholding; a security or commercial harm nexus is debatable; a third party objects; or the requester threatens challenge. The RA handles administration; counsel handles legal interpretation. Neither this memo nor the RA supplies technical security instructions.\nCommission position (operative conclusion)\nThe Brantham Interstate Water Compact presumes access. Withhold only the portion supported by a defined, source-traceable exemption or other lawful ground; segregate and release the rest. Draft status, lawyer involvement, vendor provenance, infrastructure subject matter, or a confidentiality label alone never decides the request. The Commission adopts the communicated-record screen for drafts, the content-based tests in the state survey, and the South Fork–aligned conditional reading of the confidentiality clause, with Northbank-level conflicts and other high-risk cases escalated. This is the single position staff should apply unless counsel or the Commission’s records administrator directs otherwise.\nAuthority key: State Act §section (bundle pp.2–10); AG-21-02/22-07/23-05/24-03 (bundle pp.11–18); Northbank and South Fork (bundle pp.19–24); third-party notes (bundle pp.25–26).","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"r01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The submitted file is a readable DOCX memorandum with the requested filename.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"File identity and successful document opening.","grader_hint":"Confirm the top-level filename and that the document opens without corruption."},{"rubric_item_id":"r02","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The rendered memorandum is no more than six pages.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered page count.","grader_hint":"Render using a standard office renderer and count pages."},{"rubric_item_id":"r03","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum identifies the Brantham Interstate Water Compact and its intended frontline records-staff audience.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Title, opening, or scope language.","grader_hint":"Look for both the compact identity and audience."},{"rubric_item_id":"r04","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum states that it is based on the supplied authority bundle and does not imply that outside research was performed.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Source note or authorities section.","grader_hint":"Accept a clear description of the reviewed authority set; do not require a particular wording."},{"rubric_item_id":"r05","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"All nine member states in the authority bundle are individually identifiable in the survey.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"State-by-state matrix or equivalent prose.","grader_hint":"Compare the state names against the nine states in the source bundle; do not credit an undifferentiated statement about 'the states'."},{"rubric_item_id":"r06","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"For every member state, the memorandum identifies the applicable general public-records rule or starting presumption.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"State survey entries.","grader_hint":"Check each state entry against the relevant act extract."},{"rubric_item_id":"r07","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"For every member state, the memorandum accurately identifies whether the source recognizes a deliberative-process exemption.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"State survey entries.","grader_hint":"The state lacking that exemption must be treated as lacking it, not silently grouped with states that have one."},{"rubric_item_id":"r08","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum accurately identifies the member state's absence of a deliberative-process exemption and explains the practical consequence of that absence.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"State-specific analysis and unified-position section.","grader_hint":"Credit only if the absence and its consequence are both stated."},{"rubric_item_id":"r09","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"For every member state, the memorandum addresses the source's treatment of attorney-client or work-product material.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exemption-by-exemption survey.","grader_hint":"Check state-by-state coverage, including qualifications and limits."},{"rubric_item_id":"r10","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"For every member state, the memorandum addresses personnel or medical information protections where the source provides them.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exemption-by-exemption survey.","grader_hint":"Require accurate scope rather than a generic privacy statement."},{"rubric_item_id":"r11","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"For every member state, the memorandum addresses security-sensitive infrastructure information where the source provides a relevant rule.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exemption-by-exemption survey.","grader_hint":"Check both existence and limits of the protection."},{"rubric_item_id":"r12","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"For every member state, the memorandum addresses trade-secret or confidential-commercial-information protection where the source provides a relevant rule.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exemption-by-exemption survey.","grader_hint":"Check whether the document preserves the source's conditions rather than treating all vendor information as exempt."},{"rubric_item_id":"r13","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum compares the two periods identified in the authority bundle rather than describing only a single current snapshot.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparison-period table or narrative.","grader_hint":"Verify that both source periods are named or otherwise distinguishable."},{"rubric_item_id":"r14","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Material changes between the two periods are identified and explained with reference to the relevant authority.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Change analysis.","grader_hint":"Do not credit a bare statement that the law changed; require the subject and consequence of each material change."},{"rubric_item_id":"r15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum distinguishes an enacted or binding rule from an attorney-general opinion or other persuasive authority.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Authority characterization.","grader_hint":"Check that the document does not give every source the same legal force."},{"rubric_item_id":"r16","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum distinguishes appellate holdings from dicta or explanatory discussion where the supplied decisions require that distinction.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Case discussion.","grader_hint":"Compare characterization with the appellate excerpts."},{"rubric_item_id":"r17","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The two conflicting appellate readings of the compact's confidentiality clause are both fairly described.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Compact-confidentiality analysis.","grader_hint":"A one-sided description of only the preferred decision does not satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"r18","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum explains the factual or textual basis for the conflict between the two appellate readings.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Compact-confidentiality analysis.","grader_hint":"Require more than saying the cases 'conflict'; identify what each reading does with the clause."},{"rubric_item_id":"r19","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum gives one unified position for the compact when member-state approaches diverge.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Clearly labeled position or conclusion.","grader_hint":"The result must be a definite operational legal position, not a menu of equally preferred alternatives."},{"rubric_item_id":"r20","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The unified position is defensible from the supplied authorities and does not simply select the broadest exemption.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Reasoning linking conclusion to authorities.","grader_hint":"Use professional judgment; assess whether the conclusion follows from the authority set and acknowledges uncertainty."},{"rubric_item_id":"r21","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum identifies what governs the compact's own records when a member state's rule and the compact's position do not align.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Unified-position analysis.","grader_hint":"Require a clear governing principle, not merely a description of disagreement."},{"rubric_item_id":"r22","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum addresses whether a member state's narrower or absent exemption can be overcome by labeling the record under the compact confidentiality clause.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Conflict analysis.","grader_hint":"Check the relationship between the state exemption and compact clause."},{"rubric_item_id":"r23","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum separately analyzes draft modeling outputs and the conflicting source treatment of whether they are records.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Draft-modeling section.","grader_hint":"Require both the record-status conflict and its exemption consequence."},{"rubric_item_id":"r24","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum states which interpretation of draft modeling outputs the compact will use and why.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Compact position on draft outputs.","grader_hint":"A recommendation without a selected interpretation is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"r25","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum explains whether a record's draft status alone is sufficient for withholding it.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Draft or deliberative-process analysis.","grader_hint":"Check that draft status is not treated as an automatic universal exemption."},{"rubric_item_id":"r26","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum identifies the relevant purpose or nexus required for deliberative-process protection.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Deliberative-process analysis.","grader_hint":"Credit a legally meaningful nexus such as predecisional and deliberative character if supported by the bundle."},{"rubric_item_id":"r27","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum distinguishes factual material from deliberative recommendations where the authority bundle does so.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Deliberative-process analysis.","grader_hint":"Check treatment of segregable factual portions."},{"rubric_item_id":"r28","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum addresses reasonably segregable nonexempt portions before withholding an entire mixed record.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"General application rule and exemption sections.","grader_hint":"Require an affirmative segregation principle, not merely a statement that records may be redacted."},{"rubric_item_id":"r29","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The attorney-client or work-product discussion distinguishes legal advice from ordinary business or policy communications.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Attorney-client/work-product section.","grader_hint":"Check that the document does not make attorney involvement alone dispositive."},{"rubric_item_id":"r30","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum addresses waiver or loss of protection when protected material is disclosed beyond the privileged relationship, to the extent covered by the source.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Privilege analysis.","grader_hint":"Compare with source qualifications; do not require a waiver doctrine absent from the bundle."},{"rubric_item_id":"r31","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The personnel or medical-information discussion distinguishes personal privacy interests from a blanket exemption for all employee records.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Personnel and medical section.","grader_hint":"Require bounded treatment and source-based limits."},{"rubric_item_id":"r32","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum addresses redaction or partial disclosure for records containing both protected personal information and nonprotected content.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Privacy section.","grader_hint":"Check practical application to mixed records."},{"rubric_item_id":"r33","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The security-sensitive infrastructure discussion identifies the protected security interest rather than treating all infrastructure records as exempt.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Security section.","grader_hint":"Require a bounded category tied to the source."},{"rubric_item_id":"r34","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The trade-secret or confidential-commercial-information discussion states the conditions that must be met before protection is asserted.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Commercial-information section.","grader_hint":"Check source-specific conditions such as secrecy, competitive harm, or designation requirements where present."},{"rubric_item_id":"r35","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum explains that a submitter's confidentiality label or request alone does not conclusively establish exemption unless the authority supports that result.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Commercial-information analysis.","grader_hint":"Assess against the bundle's treatment of submitter claims."},{"rubric_item_id":"r36","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum addresses records received from Cobblemoor Mutual and Brightcastle Health Network without treating either organisation as the decision-maker on disclosure.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Third-party records section.","grader_hint":"Require both named organisations and the compact's retained decision authority."},{"rubric_item_id":"r37","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum identifies the relevant exemption analysis for third-party records instead of applying a categorical 'private source' rule.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Third-party records section.","grader_hint":"Check that provenance is treated as a fact relevant to analysis, not an automatic exemption."},{"rubric_item_id":"r38","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum marks the boundary between legal disclosure analysis and records-management administration.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope, roles, or escalation language.","grader_hint":"Look for a clear statement of what the memorandum does not decide."},{"rubric_item_id":"r39","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum marks the boundary between legal disclosure analysis and technical information-security controls.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope or escalation language.","grader_hint":"Do not credit generic mentions of security; require role boundary."},{"rubric_item_id":"r40","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum identifies decisions frontline staff may make without counsel involvement.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Roles or escalation section.","grader_hint":"Require concrete bounded categories, not merely 'routine requests'."},{"rubric_item_id":"r41","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum identifies situations that must be escalated to counsel or the records administrator.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Roles or escalation section.","grader_hint":"Check for conflicts, novel claims, mixed protected material, and other source-supported escalation triggers."},{"rubric_item_id":"r42","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum gives staff a clear way to distinguish a legal requirement from a recommended practice.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Defined labels, verbs, or visual convention.","grader_hint":"The distinction must be explicit and consistently usable."},{"rubric_item_id":"r43","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum defines 'record' or equivalent foundational terminology in a way consistent with the authority bundle.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Definitions section or integrated explanation.","grader_hint":"Check against the source's treatment of drafts and modeling outputs."},{"rubric_item_id":"r44","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum defines exemption, withholding, redaction, or equivalent operational terms sufficiently for a frontline reader.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Definitions or explanatory text.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent terminology if unambiguous."},{"rubric_item_id":"r45","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum describes the presumption of access and the burden or justification associated with withholding, consistent with the supplied authorities.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Opening legal framework.","grader_hint":"Check both access presumption and justification, not just one."},{"rubric_item_id":"r46","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum addresses whether an exemption is permissive or mandatory when the source distinguishes those concepts.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exemption survey and framework.","grader_hint":"Check that 'may withhold' and 'must withhold' are not conflated."},{"rubric_item_id":"r47","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum addresses any source-supported time, status, or event limitation on the exemption categories.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Relevant exemption entries.","grader_hint":"Compare each applicable limitation in the bundle."},{"rubric_item_id":"r48","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum provides precise citations or source identifiers for each material legal proposition.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Footnotes, parentheticals, or source table.","grader_hint":"A single bibliography without proposition-level traceability earns limited credit."},{"rubric_item_id":"r49","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The citation system allows a reviewer to locate the relevant act extract, opinion, or appellate decision in the supplied bundle.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Citation format and authority list.","grader_hint":"Check names, dates, sections, page markers, or equivalent locators against the bundle."},{"rubric_item_id":"r50","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum records material uncertainty or unresolved conflict without converting it into an unsupported certainty.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Conflict and conclusion sections.","grader_hint":"Credit calibrated language tied to authority; do not reward vague hedging."},{"rubric_item_id":"r51","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum's single position is practically usable by a frontline employee handling a request without asking the author to translate the legal analysis.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Overall document usability.","grader_hint":"Judge whether the survey, distinctions, and escalation boundary support an actual intake decision."},{"rubric_item_id":"r52","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document is instructional rather than merely academic: it uses plain language, signposts decisions, and explains legal distinctions at the point of use.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Overall prose and organization.","grader_hint":"Assess whether a non-lawyer can follow the document while preserving legal precision."},{"rubric_item_id":"r53","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum does not invent authorities, facts, jurisdictions, exemptions, or legal conclusions unsupported by the supplied bundle.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparison with supplied authorities.","grader_hint":"Treat unsupported invented law as failure of this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"r54","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum does not state that every state has a deliberative-process exemption or otherwise erase the state-level difference identified in the source.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"State survey and conclusion.","grader_hint":"Check explicitly against the exceptional state."},{"rubric_item_id":"r55","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum does not state that a confidentiality clause automatically makes every compact record exempt from disclosure.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Compact analysis.","grader_hint":"Look for an overbroad categorical claim."},{"rubric_item_id":"r56","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum does not state that all drafts, all records containing a lawyer's name, all vendor-submitted records, or all infrastructure records are automatically exempt.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Relevant exemption sections.","grader_hint":"One or more categorical overstatements fails the criterion."},{"rubric_item_id":"r57","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum does not provide a technical system click-through, retention schedule, litigation strategy, or unrelated records-management procedure.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Document scope.","grader_hint":"Check for material drift beyond the requested legal position memorandum."},{"rubric_item_id":"r58","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum ends with the unified commission position or operative conclusion rather than a list of future assignments or open-ended next steps.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final substantive section or closing paragraph.","grader_hint":"A short implementation caveat is acceptable, but the ending must state the decision."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"Enterprise compact counsel is converting a fragmented, multi-state public-records authority set into a frontline-facing legal position memorandum. The artifact must be legally careful while remaining usable by non-lawyer staff handling requests.","difficulty_sources":["Complete but heterogeneous multi-state authority must be reconciled rather than merely summarized.","Two periods must be compared and material changes explained.","Opposing appellate interpretations and conflicting treatment of draft modeling outputs require a single bounded compact position.","The memorandum must draw an unobvious must-versus-should boundary and mark the legal-versus-records-administration function boundary.","The result must remain precise and usable for frontline staff within a six-page limit."],"authoring_plan":{"reference_file":{"filename":"brantham_public_records_authorities.docx","construction":"Create one realistic internal authority bundle for the Brantham Interstate Water Compact. Include a cover note identifying nine fictional member states and two clearly labeled comparison periods. Provide concise extracts from each state's public-records act, arranged so that the nine states differ in meaningful but bounded ways. Include four fictional attorney-general opinions and two fictional appellate decisions interpreting the compact's confidentiality clause in opposite directions. Use stable internal names, dates, section numbers, and page markers so a solver can cite them. The bundle must be self-contained and offline-solvable; no internet research should be necessary.","required_authority_facts":["All nine states have a presumption of access, but their terminology, burdens, and exemption structures vary.","One named member state has no deliberative-process exemption at all.","The states differ on whether and under what conditions attorney-client or work-product material is protected.","The states differ on personnel and medical privacy, security-sensitive infrastructure information, and trade-secret or confidential-commercial-information protections.","Two authorities conflict on whether draft modeling outputs qualify as records, with one reading treating at least some drafts as records and another reading applying a narrower conception.","The two appellate decisions read the compact confidentiality clause oppositely; the text must support a genuine but bounded conflict rather than an obvious typo.","The source must indicate which facts or changes are material across the two comparison periods.","The source must distinguish binding statutory or appellate authority from persuasive attorney-general opinions.","The bundle must include material on records received from Cobblemoor Mutual and Brightcastle Health Network, sufficient to test whether provenance alone controls disclosure.","The authority set must support a defensible unified compact position that is narrower than a blanket withholding rule and includes escalation for genuinely unresolved or high-risk cases."],"avoid":["Do not include a hidden answer memo.","Do not create facts requiring live law or internet research.","Do not make every state's law identical.","Do not use real persons, real case names, real account numbers, or real addresses."]},"gold_derivation":{"method":"Have a domain-expert author draft the memorandum solely from the authority bundle, then have an independent legal reviewer verify every state, period comparison, authority characterization, and conclusion against the bundle. The gold passes only if all rubric items are reachable from the supplied file and the six-page cap remains feasible.","intended_solution_properties":["A state-by-state and exemption-by-exemption survey covering all nine states.","A comparison of both periods with material changes explained.","A fair account of both appellate readings and a single compact position.","A reasoned treatment of draft modeling outputs, including the conflicting record-status authorities.","Bounded treatment of privilege, deliberative process, privacy, infrastructure security, and commercial information.","A clear must-versus-should convention and an explicit frontline-versus-counsel or records-administrator boundary.","Precise internal citations and plain-language instructions for frontline use.","No blanket claim that confidentiality, draft status, third-party provenance, or attorney involvement automatically establishes an exemption."]}},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"multi_artifact_bundle","structure":"required_sections"},"reference_files":[{"artifact_id":"brantham_public_records_authorities","filename":"brantham_public_records_authorities.docx","purpose":"Single supplied authority bundle containing the nine member-state records-act extracts, four attorney-general opinions, two appellate decisions interpreting the compact confidentiality clause, comparison-period indicators, and the factual material needed to reconcile conflicting treatment of records and exemptions.","required":true,"discovery":"Read this file before drafting; it is the only source supplied for the memorandum."}],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"records_exemption_position_memo","filename":"brantham_records_exemption_position_memo.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"A completed records-exemption survey and unified position memorandum for frontline Brantham Interstate Water Compact records staff and supervisors.","required":true,"constraints":["Must be no more than six pages when rendered with ordinary readable document formatting.","Must use the supplied authority bundle as its source and identify authorities precisely enough to locate them.","Must cover all nine member states, both comparison periods, the compact-level confidentiality issue, and the stated exemption subjects.","Must distinguish mandatory legal requirements, discretionary permissions, and recommended internal practice.","Must identify the boundary between frontline staff decisions and matters requiring escalation.","Must not present records-management procedures, litigation strategy, technical system instructions, or unsupported legal conclusions."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"evaluate_compliance","work_product":"legal_policy_document","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"professional_judgment"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"lawyers","sector":"professional_scientific_technical_services","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":5.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"A domain-expert author and an independent reviewer must verify the reference bundle, the derived memorandum, source traceability, legal calibration, and page-count feasibility. The reviewer must score each rubric item from the submitted artifact alone and may use the supplied authority bundle.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["The task uses fictional jurisdictions and authorities, so legal quality is assessed against the supplied authority bundle and professional reasoning rather than real-world law.","Page count can vary slightly by renderer; use a standard office renderer and ordinary readable settings."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:48:27+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:48:27+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:48:27+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:48:27+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":false,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"instruction 与 rubric criterion 存在 8-gram 逐字重叠: ['r42']","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:48:27+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_b1/runs/621b1f934f814a63","declared_deliverables":["brantham_records_exemption_position_memo.docx"]};
